  "response": "A failure investigation of this kind begins by treating the oil chemistry and the electrical record as two lines of evidence that answer different questions, and the discipline of the exercise lies in interpreting each on its own terms before they are brought together. Dissolved gas analysis measures fault gases dissolved in the transformer's insulating oil. Elevated carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide levels indicate degradation of cellulose paper insulation. Measurement of the degree of polymerization on paper samples quantifies cellulose aging. On a separate laboratory point, the degree of polymerization of new kraft insulating paper is approximately 200.\n\nThe electrical side of the investigation rests on waveform capture at the transformer terminals, which is where any imported disturbance must first appear. Digital fault recorders capture terminal voltage and current waveforms at sub-cycle resolution. Lightning strikes and capacitor-bank switching generate steep-fronted overvoltage transients on the network, and such steep fronts in turn concentrate voltage stress across the first turns of the winding. Because the recorder fixes each event to a fraction of a cycle, time-stamping the fault recorder event against the DGA sampling history establishes the sequence of events, and a chronology is precisely what an argument about origin requires.\n\nA DGA trend showing gradual gas accumulation over several years indicates a pre-existing internal defect. That the gas record speaks only to the existence and history of such a defect rules out any claim that dissolved gas analysis locates a winding fault to within ten millimetres of its physical position. Such a years-long accumulation of gas confirms that the paper insulation of the winding had aged beyond its end-of-life criterion. The recorded waveform, for its part, shows that the incoming surge at the transformer terminals exceeded the rated impulse withstand level; at least one of these two conditions, exhausted paper or an over-withstand surge, obtained at the moment of failure, and perhaps both did. The exhausted condition of the paper is itself the evidence that shows the failure initiated within the transformer's internal insulation system, since a dielectric already below its end-of-life criterion will break down at stresses a sound winding would tolerate.\n\nThat conclusion can be restated plainly: the origin of the failure lay inside the transformer rather than on the external network. By contrast with the over-withstand surge measured at the terminals, which arrived from the system side of the bushings, the weakness that gave way belonged to the transformer itself. The surge therefore enters the account as the stress that acted on an already degraded dielectric, and the practical consequences follow from where initiation occurred rather than from the magnitude of the transient: the remedy lies in replacement or rewind of the unit, not in network-side mitigation alone. Where the two datasets appear to point in different directions, the apparent conflict dissolves once each is read for what it can establish, the chemistry for condition and history and the waveform for the instantaneous electrical environment, and once the two are ordered in time.",
